\description{
Get the per-game drive log for a single college football game
-- one row per drive, with start/end field position, yardage, play count,
result, and a \verb{$ref} to that drive's plays. Optionally embed each drive's
full-schema play-by-play as a nested list-column or as a flat
one-row-per-play table.
}
\details{
Wraps the ESPN core-v2 endpoint
\code{events/{game_id}/competitions/{game_id}/drives}. The competition id always
equals the game id. Returns one row per drive (typically 18-24 drives per
game). Each drive carries the offensive team, the start and end
period/clock/yard-line, total yards, offensive play count, the drive
result, and a scoring flag. The \code{plays_ref} column is the \verb{$ref} URL to
that drive's plays -- pass the \code{drive_id} to \code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_drive_plays]{espn_cfb_game_drive_plays()}}
to fetch the play-by-play partitioned to a single drive. This is the
richest drive resource ESPN exposes; the site-v2 summary feed only carries
a flattened subset.

The \code{plays} argument controls whether each drive's plays are embedded:
\itemize{
\item \code{"none"} (default) -- the drives output is returned unchanged,
one row per drive, with no embedded plays.
\item \code{"list"} -- a \code{plays} \strong{list-column} is appended; each cell is
that drive's plays as a tibble in the \strong{full}
\code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_pbp]{espn_cfb_game_pbp()}} schema (~54 base columns plus any optional
participant columns). A drive with no matched plays gets an empty
0-row tibble, never \code{NULL}.
\item \code{"expand"} -- the \strong{flat one-row-per-play} table is returned:
every play in the full \code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_pbp]{espn_cfb_game_pbp()}} schema, with that
play's drive-level columns carried alongside, prefixed \code{drive_}
(e.g. \code{drive_id}, \code{drive_result}, \code{drive_yards},
\code{drive_start_yard_line}) to avoid collision with the play's own
columns. This is the \code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_pbp]{espn_cfb_game_pbp()}} expanded form enriched
with drive context.
}

The \code{participants}, \code{participants_list}, \code{team_participants},
\code{team_participants_list}, and \code{position_detail} arguments shape the
embedded plays and are consulted \strong{only} when \code{plays != "none"}. They
have the same meaning as the identically named arguments on
\code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_pbp]{espn_cfb_game_pbp()}} -- e.g. \code{participants = "wide"} appends the
type-keyed \verb{\{type\}_player_*} columns to every embedded play tibble. See
\code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_pbp]{espn_cfb_game_pbp()}} for the full description of each.

To get the plays efficiently the wrapper first probes the drives
endpoint for inline plays (\verb{items[].plays.items[]}); when ESPN embeds
them, no extra request is made. When only a \code{plays.$ref} is present, the
competition-level \verb{/plays} feed is fetched \strong{once} and the plays are
partitioned into drives by matching each play's drive id. Per-drive
\code{plays_ref} URLs are never dereferenced individually.

\code{\link[=espn_cfb_unnest_plays]{espn_cfb_unnest_plays()}} turns a \code{plays = "list"} result into the same
flat table as \code{plays = "expand"} without any further HTTP.
}
